ingle eigenvalue $1$. How should the quadratic form $\\boldsymbol{x}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}$ be classified?","options":["Indefinite","Positive definite","Positive semidefinite but not positive definite","It cannot be classified, as $\\boldsymbol{A}$ is not symmetric"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b75547a8064c295765d7aa94","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_definiteness_and_eigenvalues_aa2e8551_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A real symmetric $2\\times2$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ is known only through $\\det\\boldsymbol{A}=6$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(\\boldsymbol{A})=-5$. Which definiteness class does $\\boldsymbol{A}$ belong to?","options":["Positive definite","Indefinite","It cannot be decided without the eigenvalues","Negative definite"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4145097142d1cfb42b46d9be","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_definiteness_and_eigenvalues_aa2e8551_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A real symmetric $2\\times2$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has eigenvalues $-1$ and $4$. As $\\boldsymbol{x}$ ranges over all unit vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ (vectors with $\\boldsymbol{x}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{x}=1$), which set of values does $\\boldsymbol{x}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}$ take?","options":["Only the two values $-1$ and $4$","Every value in the closed interval $[-1,4]$","Every value in the open interval $(-1,4)$","Every real number, since $\\boldsymbol{A}$ is indefinite"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f3e1ab8e242560078296f349","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_definiteness_and_eigenvalues_aa2e8551_fill220261112_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A real symmetric $3\\times3$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has eigenvalues $-1$, $2$ and $3$. For which real numbers $c$ is $\\boldsymbol{A}+c\\boldsymbol{I}$ positive definite?","options":["Exactly for $c>1$","Exactly for $c\\ge1$","Exactly for $c>-1$","Exactly for $c>3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"606589265fb4cf3ec18000d3","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_definiteness_and_eigenvalues_aa2e8551_fill220261112_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A real symmetric $n\\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ is positive semidefinite and invertible. What can be concluded about its definiteness?","options":["It is positive semidefinite, but whether it is positive definite cannot be decided.","It is positive definite exactly when all of its entries are positive.","It is positive definite.","It need not be positive definite: invertibility excludes $\\det\\boldsymbol{A}=0$, not a zero eigenvalue."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5dbfb291848dc404b55469f0","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_definiteness_and_eigenvalues_aa2e8551_fill220261112_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A real symmetric $2\\times2$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has eigenvalue $4$ with eigenvector $(1,1)$ and eigenvalue $9$ with eigenvector $(1,-1)$. The set of $\\boldsymbol{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ with $\\boldsymbol{x}^{\\top}\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}=1$ is an ellipse centred at the origin. What is its longer half-axis?","options":["Half-length $\\tfrac{1}{3}$, along $(1,-1)$","Half-length $3$, along $(1,-1)$","Half-length $\\tfrac{1}{4}$, along $(1,1)$","Half-length $\\tfrac{1}{2}$, along $(1,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"696f19d2faf1a58539470335","time_limit":40}]}}